    <table:table-row table:style-name="TableLine94660335881296">
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2.A3" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="P121">Traits</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table2.D1" table:number-columns-spanned="3" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="P115"><text:span text:style-name="T162">[Claws] [Foxfire] </text:span>[Hekate’s Blessing] <text:span text:style-name="T138">[Kitsune Blood] [Ruptured Manapool] [Weapon Handling] </text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:covered-table-cell/>
     <table:covered-table-cell/>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P151" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 8</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T12"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T16">Are those beast suppressors?” Jack asks when we get to breakfast.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“They are.” I say showing off the trinkets, “It’s my new jewelry.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“Wait, that whole tainted by beast spirits thing is true? That can actually happen?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>I take my seat and Merin decides to sit with me and Alex today. “No, that’s just an excuse to put people under low grade mind control. These things just prevent the wearer from getting angry. They do nothing against possessions.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“What are you an expert on enchantments now?” Greg asks.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“I can feel their effects and,” I say holding up a claw. Some fur sprouts from my hand before I revert it back to normal. “They have no reaction to possessions.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T16"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17">So you </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T18">really </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17">were the kitsune.” Mitch says, “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T18">Either way, those cuffs will keep you in your place</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17">.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T18">He and his team take that as the end of the conversation and turn back to their food. Alex keeps looking between me and Mi</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19">t</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T18">ch though.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T18"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19">A piece of egg bounces off my plate. Greg is the only one paying attention when he sends another piece at me. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20">Looking at the piece of food I can dislike for the situation but there is no real heat or desire to do something about it. Breakfast ends without me finishing my meal before I realize how much this is weighing on my mind.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T20"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T21">Randel has another surprise for us when we return to the yard. “You lot are moving up a level in your training. This is an engram crystal. They allow the transfer of ability between people. This one will unlock a new feature of your status: Health Point. Your HP will absorb any damage dealt to you preventing injuries from slowing you down in battle. Losing all of them won’t kill you outright but a stiff breeze is enough to do you in at that point.” He smirks, “Even more important, it provides an easy way to train the hardest to level attribute: Constitution.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P69"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T168">You unlocked Health Points!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P140">[HP Barrier (Hidden)]</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P140">A passive barrier absorbs all physical damage that would be done to you. All damage absorbed this way depletes a common pool of points and when that pool empties your effective constitution hits zero. Constitution increases the size of the HP pool. Endurance increase the rate points regenerate.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T168">Well, this is interesting. This crystal is doing a lot more than I expected. Watching it work so closely makes it clear that it is a hack. It even changes the System messages and tries to hide the very skill it teaches. Someone went to a lot of trouble to make this thing seem like a intended part of the wider System. It is a shame they did not put that effort into making a better skill ability though. </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T168">Redirecting damage into the System itself is strange idea of defense. It does come with a neat little meter in the corner of my vision.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T170">Randel’s idea of constitution training is live steel spars with healing sessions between. HP makes it feasible but it is still as painful as it sounds. Greg seems to enjoy it and made a point of choosing me as a partner as many times as possible. After an hour or so it is clear that HP does nothing to prevents injury but not pain. By the time we make it back to the suite I just want to lay down and cry. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:span text:style-name="T170"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">“Your base Constitution increased by 1!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:tab/>“Your base Dexterity increased by 1!”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:span text:style-name="T10"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T170">“Are you OK?” Alex asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T171">Not really.” I mumble with my head down on the breakfast table. Now that we are back in the suite there is no reason to avoid moping. Usually I sulk more than mope but these bracelets prevent that. “I feel so weak.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T172">I can’t believe they made you wear those shackles!” Alex says, “We have to find a way to get rid of them!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T172">It’s more than that, but it’s worth a check.” I say and slip the bracelets off. Shackles do not work well on shape shifters. A stream of rage starts only to die before it can build up momentum. There is no lingering magic but the new perspective is here to stay.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T172">You can just take them off.” Alex gapes, “Why didn’t you remove them sooner?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T172">They aren’t the main problem.” I sigh, “We can’t win a fight against the people here. They’re ridiculously strong but they’re also set in their ways. To just casually undermine the will of an entire race for the sake of ‘safety?’ There is no point in negotiating with them.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T173">You’re giving up.” Alex purses her lips.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T173">Before she gets caught up in her thoughts I state, “No, but I misjudged things. Living in New York for so long spoiled me a little. I’ve forgotten what its like to live in less egalitarian societies. The fact of the matter is we are not equal here. We have no standing. There is no one to complain to. The only options we have in the end are obedience or violence.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T174">And that’s why beastkin have to wear their suppressors.” Merin scolds, “They will suppress those violent urges.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T174">I shrug and put my accessories back on. It does not change much but judging by the expressions Alex is going through she disagrees. “And you choose obedience?” she asks after schooling her expression.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“I… <text:span text:style-name="T174">think I made a mistake.</text:span>” <text:span text:style-name="T174">These suppressors are great for self reflection it turns out. They weaken my sense of pride and now I realize spite is motivating me. I want to toy with them, find how </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T174">they work and slowly break them. It is a good plan if I was alone. “I’m sorry, you’re right. I have been too passive. Merin, what’s outside this fortress?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T175">The town of Edgewood, why?” Merin replies. A wooded area nearby is perfect and we do not even need to go through town to get to it.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T176">I haven’t seen any guards. No servants either for that matter.” I say, “Do you want to just leave, Alex?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T177">What? Are you serious?” Alex questions.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T178">Yeah. Nobody is really checking on us. Might as well try.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T178">And where do you think you’re going to go?” Merin asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T178">Home.” I state and get up. The boys turn down the invitation to join as we head out of the room. Not a single person shows up on our way to the training yard gate. The gate however is locked and barred. It is a massive wooden thing and the locks are magical but the iron hinges are exposed.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T179">Alright, its time to go back. Come- What are you doing!?” Merin shrieks when she sees me applying flames to a hinge. I probably could melt them outright but rusting is close enough and easier for my magic. “Stop!” She orders and reaches for my fire covered hand only to yelp and pull back when she gets brunt. “You burnt me.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“Of course. I’m not going to protect you from my fire.” I say as the first hinge crumbles apart and get to work on the next.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T180">But I have HP…” she says and we both stare at her hand. Her skin is an angry red typical of a light burn but she right. Her HP should prevent local injuries.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T181">Looks like it didn’t work.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T182">“This is serious. HP is enforced by the system. If you can bypass it then…” Merin’s gaze turns cold at that and she orders, “Come. This is a matter for the administrators. We need to report this.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T183">No, I don’t think I want to meet these administrators.” I hope she does not press the issue, but it seems interfering with the System is a red line here.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T183">I wasn’t asking.” she says and grabs my arm.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T183">I see. I’m sorry then.” My claws carve through her neck. As an actual predator, killing in cold blood is part of my nature. All these suppressors really do is remove my desire to warn people. I do wonder if that would have even made a difference as I watch the girl bleed out. As a final courtesy I wait until she stops moving to ignite her body. Alex follows me in silence through the gate with an ashen face and we disappear into the nearby forest. A glance is all the attention I pay to the adjacent town of Edgewood.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T184">We stop once the edge of the forest is no longer visible. At this point we are already out of the world. Aligning with Hekate allows me to travel along metaphorical paths. If we continue on I can lead us to Hekate’s garden and from there to Central Park. Before that though we need to clear the air.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T185">You killed her.” Alex says looking haunted, “Why? Why did you kill her?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T185">I sigh, “There is no good answer to that. She was simply an enemy.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T186">But she didn’t really do anything! You didn’t have to kill her!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T20"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T22">She works for the people that kidnapped us. She tried to control me </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">through these bracelets. Incompetence does not absolve her of trying to enslave me. You don’t have to agree with me though.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T23"><text:tab/>“Will you kill me too?” Alex asks in the grip of panic. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T24">Now I see that she is locked in a memory. That is not really a better situation but it is simpler to deal with.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T24"><text:tab/>I look to the sky in and ask, “Does the sky look strange to you?” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T25">There are no stars or moon in the empty night sky. That is normal in Hekate’s realm but should work as a distraction. A few more non <text:s/>sequiturs draw Alex’s focus back to reality. “Do you want a hug?” I ask with open arms.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:tab/>Only a moment of hesitation before Alex’s shaking form leans against me. “He killed me.” she sobs into my shoulder. As much as I would like to know who ‘he’ is but that is not what matters now. I stay silent and lower us to the a sitting position. We can stay here for a while.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"/>
