   <text:p text:style-name="P76"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P152" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 9</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P77"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T12">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26">You didn’t answer the question.” Alex points out after calming down.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T187">That’s true. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T188">No, I have no intention to kill you and I don’t see that changing.” I say but this is a tricky situation. My words do nothing to change her expression. The issue goes much deeper than simple reassurances can reach. I sigh, “You don’t have to trust me. We’ll be parting ways soon enough. I plan to stay in Prahoria but we’re already headed to Earth now.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“Oh.” Alex whispers and looks <text:span text:style-name="T190">afraid, “I think my boyfriend might have killed me.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T189">Oh.” I say before the realizing the full context. I have faked my own death so many times that restarting my life is routine for me. Losing friends still hurts but its manageable. I doubt that is the case for most people. The fact that she was murdered makes it a complete non starter. “Damn, I don’t see any good options here. You can come with me or I can set you up back on Earth, but that doesn’t really reach the heart of the issue.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T190">I don’t know who to trust.” she says and pulls her knees closer to her chest.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T190">I’m sorry, but that’s not something I can decide for you. It’s a tough choice but you don’t need to figure it out now. We have time.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T191">Alex quirks an eyebrow after a moment and looks around, “But what if they find the body? We aren’t very far away.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T191">They can’t follow us. I… bent, for lack of a better word, our path. This isn’t the forest outside the barracks. It isn’t a particular forest at all. This is a liminal space on the edge of reality attached to all forests but a part of none of them. A perk of following Hekate. Actually, we’ll be meeting her soon and she might have some advice.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T192">Some time passes before we set out again. Alex is listless but at least focused on the journey. That is good progress in my opinion. It is a suspicious sort of luck that meeting Hekate is turning into a crossroads for her life. I hope Alex is prepared to make a decision.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T193">The transition starts subtle with a few signs the forest is curated here before the trees start thinning. Soon beds of various herbs and flowers dot the surroundings with plenty of space for easy passage. The area has an air of controlled chaos much like the ruler of this demesne. Hekate stood looking over the plants wearing her typical purple toga. She stood a head taller than everyone with the appearance of a grandmother without a single wrinkle. Goddesses like to wear contradictions like jewelry.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T194">Nearly 600 years old and curiousity is still getting the better of you.” Hekate says before turning around and walking up to me with an exasperated expression, “At least you didn’t let the cursed shackles bind properly. They are normally irremovable.” She holds up the bracelets that are no longer on my wrists.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T194">It does not take long for my mind to realize what she is talking about. My ability to feel anger is restored and not happy about my recent actions. I was too hasty, too sloppy, too careless. I did not expect anger of all things to be a restraint of sorts. All I can do now is stare at the ground and make apologies.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T195">Best you make the most of the lesson since it’s cost is already paid. If you still want to learn about these shackles do it from the outside. Despite the name this is not a calming enchantment.” Hekate says and hands me the shackles with a pat on the head. She moves on to Alex as her words click into place. They remove anger but leave behind fear. The shackles of paranoia are staying off.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T196">Welcome, Alex to my garden. I am Hekate, goddess of Witchery, Necromancy and Crossroads.” <text:s/>Hekate proclaims only to relax and continue, “Bah, I’ve never been one for pageantry. Just Kate is fine. You have a interesting opportunity and difficult choice coming up but why rush it. Let’s go get some cookies.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P66"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T197">Uh, what?” Alex asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P67"><text:tab/>“Cookies, dear. Warm and delicious ones with some creamy milk. We’re going to go eat some because they make things better.” Hekate explains and heads off, “Well, come on now. You could stand there to the end of time if you want but that’s a rather boring choice.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P67"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T198">Alex blinks and looks like she may take Hekate up on that last offer as the goddess walks off. I nudge her and say, “It’s easiest to just go with the flow and figure things out when you have more information.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T199">We only go far enough to see a cottage that was just out of sight. The glow of a fire spills out of the windows and plants cover much of the surface. A carved wooden table sits outside with three chairs and a plate of cookies waiting. A glass of milk is present for each setting.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T200">After taking our seats Hekate waits long enough to see that I am the only one eating the cookies before saying, “Alright, we’ll get right to it then. Alex dear, you are a revenant. Technically an undead, but a very well crafted one in your case. The biggest complication is that you died over a year ago.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T200">Does that mean I can’t go home?” Alex worried.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T200">You can return to your previous home, but whether there is still a place for you is another matter. You have made a friend here. It is possible to make a new home here. I would love to see what kind of witch you might become.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T201">I- Thank you for the offer.” Alex says, “But I really just want to get back to my life.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T201">Hekate sighs and snaps her fingers. A freestanding door behind Alex creaks open a few inches to the woman’s obvious surprise. “That door will take you near your parents house. Your own is unavailable but this should be enough to get you going.” Hekate says and slides a credit card toward Alex, “Time is disconnected here so you can stay without missing anything on Earth. Going down a path doesn’t have to mean you’re stuck on it.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T202">I’m sorry…” Alex pauses before getting up.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T202">You don’t need to explain. I do want you to stay, but you don’t need to live your life on the whims of others.” I say, “Take care of yourself out there.” My eyes linger on the door after she closes it behind her. Even in the best case scenario Alex will have to rebuild her entire life. “I didn’t expect you to try and take her in, Kate.” I comment after realizing the door is not disappearing.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T202">Hah, of course I’m interested in the possibility of an undead witch that already has a familiar.” Hekate smirks as her word click into place in my mind.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T203">Huh. I guess I did do that.” I say and lean back to consider the implications. I did form that magical connection with Alex. This is not the direction I expected but it makes sense. “She’s coming back, right?” I ask while eyeing the door.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T204">She is. It’s just a question of time.” Hekate replies, “Ares never actually put aging into the ‘summoning’ ritual. There’s just another ritual to send the lucky heroes back home. He just left out that home is the river Styx, not Earth.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P77"><text:span text:style-name="T12"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">I take a moment to recall the name, “The god of war? I guess the system is very combat focused. We’re invading another god’s world though?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“Not really. He’s abandoned it. Figured he could just set up an eternal war and create a system to collect and refine the combat experience.” Hekate scoffs, “As if humans are going to simply act according to plan like that. He gave up after a few tries to fix things.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T205">But only left behind an interesting mess.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T205">It’s only got the potential to be interesting. Leftover heroes have too much of a strangle hold on things right now.” Hekate says before turning to the door, “Ah, your friend almost made it a week.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T205">The door creeks open to reveal Alex. She looks okay but the stiff movements belie that appearance. Any appropriate words would break the facade she is clinging to so I just nod and pull out her chair. Ales sits and after a moment takes one of the cookies. She stares at it before biting into the treat with tears welling in her eyes. “Did you know?” she asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">No, but I have a guess.” I reply, “Resurrection is too much for people to believe.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">Why didn’t you stop me?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">How?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">You could have done something!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">Alex, there were no good options.” I state with finality, “You died. You lost your life. That is a wound greater than most people ever experience. The pain and suffering were set in stone at that moment.” Alex is not happy with that response but has no follow up.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T207">After many tears and a whole plate of cookies she asks, “So what’s it like being a witch?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P77"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P77"><text:soft-page-break/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P64"/>
